Observer Pattern – Short Notes
The Observer pattern is a behavioral design pattern where an object (the subject) maintains a list of dependents (called observers) and notifies them automatically of state changes. 
Used for event-driven communication within a single application. 
Tight coupling: The subject knows its observers. 
Typically synchronous, but can be made asynchronous. 
Common in GUI frameworks, MVC/MVVM architectures, and real-time updates (e.g., stock tickers, live scoreboards).


Observer Pattern vs Pub/Sub – Comparison Table
Feature	Observer Pattern	Pub/Sub Architecture
Coupling	Tightly coupled — subject knows observers	Loosely coupled — no knowledge between publisher and subscriber
Communication	Direct method calls	Indirect via message broker (e.g., Kafka, RabbitMQ)
Knowledge of Recipients	Subject maintains a list of observers	Publishers and subscribers are unaware of each other
Scope	In-process (same application)	Cross-process or distributed systems
Delivery	Usually synchronous	Usually asynchronous
Scalability	Limited to single app	Highly scalable
Fault Tolerance	Observer failure may affect subject	Broker ensures message durability
Filtering	Observers filter events locally	Broker supports topic/content-based filtering
Use Cases	UI updates, model-view sync	Microservices, event streaming, logging
